hello and welcome to the Daily climate  show coming up today kicking away carbon  the unconvincing claims from FIFA and  the  and those tournament will be carbon  neutral  a bittersweet ending to cop27 in Egypt  with a deal to help the poorest country  suffering climate disasters but without  a commitment to cut carbon emissions  and a warning that despite China's  greenhouse gas emissions in Decline the  country is yet to reach its peak  [Music]  foreign  to the Daily climate show where we track  the changes to our world and interrogate  some of the potential solutions to The  Climate crisis now if you're watching  this today football might not be your  cup of tea but FIFA and the hosts of  this year's World Cup Qatar insisted the  tournament will be completely carbon  neutral a claim causing uproar and  thought by many to be extremely  misleading but what exactly is carbon  neutrality well essentially it means  taking steps to remove the equivalent  amount of CO2 to what's emitted through  activities like building stadiums by  investing in carbon sinks such as  forests and oceans that absorb CO2 now  carbon offsetting is the main method of  achieving this a way of paying for  others to reduce emissions or absorb CO2  to compensate for your own for example  by planting trees or supplying energy  efficient equipment elsewhere now Net  Zero is similar in principle but on a  much larger scale meaning all greenhouse  gases being emitted into the atmosphere  such as methane are balanced out well  our climate change and energy  correspondent Hannah Thomas Peter joins  me now I mean this is a major sporting  event is it even possible well Qatar is  insisting that it is but it is being  accused of some really creative carbon  accounting here that's why carbon market  watch was a very well respected  independent Think Tank he said it has  serious doubts about the claims just  doesn't pass the common sense test  because if you look at the scale of what  qatars had to create from scratch there  have been millions of tons of carbon  emitted so you think about seven of the  eight stadiums created from scratch all  of the new infrastructure transporting  and housing  fans and players many of whom of course  flew in with the extra emissions  associated with that now Qatar is saying  look we've got solar powered Cooling in  the in the stadiums and we recycled lots  of materials now in in the way we've  made our stadiums in particular but FIFA  is saying that the entire Enterprise is  going to create 3.6 million tons of  carbon Qatar is saying it's going to  offset a lot of that that is a deeply  problematic approach to Net Zero the  carbon Market in general is not very  well regulated in this case a fraction  of the necessary carbon credits have  been purchased a lot of them have been  purchased in renewable energy which was  going to reduce carbon emissions anyway  so there's no additionality which is a  key test of carbon credits and a lot of  the carbon credits have been purchased  not through an independent third party  which is how the vast majority of the  rest of the world does it but through an  entity created by Qatar itself now this  country is not alone in creating  millions of tons of emissions in  relation to a big sporting event it  happened in Russia the World Cup you  know in in 2018 and then in 2016 in the  Rio Olympics created 4.5 million tons of  carbon emissions but Qatar is the only  nation that has claimed carbon  neutrality and that is why it is being  so closely scrutinized and accused of  greenwashing okay Hannah thank you very  much so so interesting  um well let's take a look at some of the  days other climate news for you now  because China's greenhouse gas emissions  have been in Decline since last year  that's according to a new report that's  warned although this may seem like  positive news there could still be some  way until the country reaches its peak  well China's policies are still not  fully aligned with long-term goals to  limit temperature increases and while  the country is doing well in areas like  clean energy and electric vehicles new  research suggests it's still off track  in the coal fire powered Iron and Steel  area now the amount of palm oil being  exported out of Indonesia has risen by  10.3 percent in the last year to last  year Indonesia produced 46 million tons  of crude palm oil making it the world's  largest palm oil producer now producing  the crop contributes to global warming  and continues to be a major Drive in  deforestation in some of the most  bio-device forests around the world  now a new study has warned that more  than half of England's estuaries are  under threat of climate change now  Estuary is a bodies of water usually  found where rivers meet the sea and a  key for maintaining Wildlife such as  wading birds and acts as breeding  grounds for fish but scientists say a  combination of rising Seas Lower River  levels and man-made barriers such as  Weirs and dams are putting fresh water  habitats in estuaries at risk  okay now after hours of late night  negotiations uh the cop 27 climate  Summit finally came to an end a day  later than expected and the final  results an agreement on loss and damage  a dedicated fund to help the poorest  countries suffering the worst effects of  climate change so almost 200 countries  signed up to the landmark deal an  example is Pakistan following its recent  catastrophic flooding but there was no  agreement on cutting carbon emissions  the root cause of those climate  disasters  I'm happy that it it it it worked out  I'm happy it got gaveled that didn't get  blocked in the night or  we built guard rails we were you know we  were tents for two nights we haven't  slept so yeah it may not be the best  it's it's the beginning it's a  tremendous outcome we've been fighting  for this for three decades and the group  has been putting forward many proposals  and the world has come together I've  been very clear in my comments that I'm  disappointed we couldn't go further than  Glasgow yeah and that's something that's  being echoed by other parties  particularly climate vulnerable  countries the only way you can get us  where we need to be is by quickly  reducing emissions and that's the only  way you can stay on on the 1.5 Celsius  trajectory and I have not seen progress  on that what we have actually seen is  increased investment increased  commitment in terms of Renewables yes  there's been backsliding in terms of  fossil fuel use in response to  um to to the energy crisis  well joining me now is Nicola day Deputy  head of rathbourne green Bank  Investments and Frankie Oliver founder  of new Society ladies thank you very  much for joining us  um so you would have heard there some of  the world leaders their reaction to that  deal made in cop uh cop 27 in Egypt I  just want to get your thoughts on this  do you feel Frankie that cop went far  enough or did it fail to hit the mark in  terms of that loss and damage fund  agreed  well it's obviously excellent we have  some agreements around the loss and  damage fund but there is no  um framework around how that fund is  actually going to be administered and as  far as Corp is concerned it is failing  us as a process in terms of actually  dealing with the catastrophic  consequences of the climate crisis we  know we're near uh delivering the 1.5  outcome and I expect really that 1.5 is  no longer alive  uh Nicola be good to get your thoughts  on that because some people are saying  you know the deal was the biggest  Advance since the Paris agreement at  cop15 what do you think  well I agree certainly it was it was  very mixed it was it was good to get  that headline announcement on the loss  and damage financing it was the first  time we had financing for loss and  damage on the agenda but I agree that it  didn't go far enough you know we wanted  much more in terms of the language  regarding fossil fuels  um and also you know we do know  commitments need to really ratchet up  we've heard from the scientists over the  last two weeks who are really talking  with much more  urgency now you know we're heading for  sort of in between 2.4 and 2.8 by the  end of the century which will bring  catastrophic change so we absolutely  realized that Ambitions need to raise  enormously from here and that wasn't in  the the final text  but I do think you know it's a it's a  cop where we've seen two outcomes not  only just those headline figures but we  have seen a lot of uh collaboration on  the ground and I think that's where  we're releasing some of the positive  sides of of cop27 coming out and it's  interesting around language because that  is quite powerful but there was no in  the text at least no commitment to  phasing out fossil fuels Nicola what do  you think of that  yes there certainly wasn't the phasing  out it was still stuck to the language  of phasing down and it was only going to  fossil fuels um in that limited sort of  coal side so we didn't have the full  fossil fuels and the and the language is  still phasing down not phasing out so  definitely we don't think the language  is aggressive enough at the top and  actually you know having been at cop27  from for most of those um two weeks it  wasn't really reflecting a lot of the  language we were hearing on the ground  there is no more ambition on the ground  but these final texts they have to bring  together the consensus from nearly 200  countries it's a very hard process and I  think you know it it's important we just  keep on nudging forward and and trying  to make sure that these steps are bigger  each time at cop but but the fossil fuel  side was disappointing I agree uh  frankly I know you have a nine-year-old  for you after this cop how do you feel  about the future  well I feel deeply concerned I mean I  know that  um by the time my nine-year-old hits 20  it's more than likely that all coral  reef will be dead  um it's more than likely that the  Greenland I teach is going to collapse  which is going to cause catastrophic  floods and droughts Etc around the world  um this is going to cause food shortages  water shortages Mass migration  geopolitical risk I mean I think the  situation is absolutely horrific and I  think what we know from these  conferences I mean we have agreement  unilateral agreement from 197 countries  at Paris in 2015 and we're still  absolutely nowhere near dealing with  this climate crisis the ndc's that all  countries were asked to submit for their  27 only 24 of the 197 have actually  submitted and we've only cut the 45  admissions Target that we need to  achieve by 2030 by one percent so  everybody listening to this really needs  to take a minute and consider just how  grave a situation we are in and how  these processes are simply not tackling  the climate crisis and to both of you  very briefly then because the message  here is that we've had almost 30 years  of cop talks the next one being in Dubai  20 cop 28 are they working very briefly  Nicola  I think they're they're working from the  point of view they are an important part  of climate action they really are the  point where you have the the country's  under scrutiny to raise their Ambitions  and it's a a great environment where you  do see this collaboration on the ground  you know really initiatives being  launched between policy makers the  private the public sectors initiative  strength and collaborations formed lots  of deals done lots of knowledge sharing  and that's really the power of cop as  well as these headlines and Frankie do  you think they should continue or is  there another way of making improvements  and agreements in the future very  briefly we're running out of time  I think they do show a transparency  around where we are and I understand  that there's collaboration on the ground  and so forth but that's two weeks and we  need 52 weeks  um to really deliver the outcome that we  need year on year and we absolutely need  to be reporting back on that you know  more regularly than just every year  okay Nicola day and Frankie Oliver thank  you very much for your time and insights  today just a reminder that you can watch  Tom heat show on Saturdays and Sundays  at 3 30 in the afternoon or in the  evening at 7 30 and thanks very much for  watching today we'll be back tomorrow  with more

